WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.051
Which traces to review: sampling production traffic.

2
00:00:03.331 --> 00:00:06.872
You can’t read everything, so the question is which traces to read.

3
00:00:07.152 --> 00:00:14.427
The evals FAQ lists sampling strategies from most exploratory to most targeted, and each has a blind spot.

4
00:00:14.707 --> 00:00:19.144
Random sampling gives you an unbiased picture but can miss rare failures.

5
00:00:19.424 --> 00:00:25.995
Clustering traces by topic or embedding shows you groups you didn’t know existed, but depends on how you cluster.

6
00:00:26.275 --> 00:00:33.613
Outliers in latency, cost, or number of tool calls are cheap to find, though an unusual trace isn’t always a bad one.

7
00:00:33.893 --> 00:00:39.589
Classifier or judge flags find failures you already know how to detect, and nothing else.

8
00:00:39.869 --> 00:00:43.539
Negative user feedback finds what users bothered to report.

9
00:00:43.819 --> 00:00:57.515
The FAQ’s recommendation is to mix them: keep some random traces in every batch, so you keep measuring the overall rate and keep finding the unknown, and add targeted traces to dig into rare or known failure modes.

10
00:00:57.795 --> 00:01:06.755
Its rough cadence for a live product is a fresh batch of at least 100 traces every two to four weeks, plus a small weekly look at outliers.

11
00:01:07.035 --> 00:01:13.221
Stop a review round at theoretical saturation: when new traces stop revealing new failure types.

12
00:01:13.501 --> 00:01:16.296
Keep the two purposes separate in your analysis.

13
00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:24.235
The random slice is your measurement: its pass rate, with a confidence interval from week 3, is your estimate of production quality.

14
00:01:24.515 --> 00:01:32.664
The targeted slices are for discovery: they are deliberately enriched with failures, so their pass rate says nothing about overall quality.

15
00:01:32.944 --> 00:01:38.320
Mixing them into one number is a classic way to scare or reassure yourself for no reason.

16
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.624
Two more rules.

17
00:01:39.904 --> 00:01:44.341
Sample from the traffic you actually care about (filter out internal testing).

18
00:01:44.621 --> 00:01:55.629
And watch privacy: production traces contain customer data, so reviewers need the same access controls as anyone else who sees it, and you should redact what the review doesn’t need.
